{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-43115",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ k & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7k+23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $z$ is given by $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ k & -4 & -19 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & -19 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -19 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-16-19)-(4k+19)+7(-k+4) \\\\  & =-70-4k-19-7k+28 \\\\  & =-61-11k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-61-11k}{7k+23}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $z=-2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-61-11k}{7k+23} & =-2 \\\\ -61-11k & =-14k-46 \\\\ 3k & =15 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
